Author: askmeaboutloom <askmeaboutlo0m@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 13 15:23:22 2026 +0200

    [android] Handle edge-to-edge by not doing it
    
    Under Android 16 (API level 36, Baklava), edge-to-edge display is
    enforced. That means Android will always spill the application view into
    nonsensical areas like underneath the notification bar, the navigation
    bar or the camera notch, with no OS-level way to opt out of it.
    
    This commit deals with it by returning the behavior to how it was
    before, adding blank borders around the areas where those elements are.
    This only gets applied on Android 16, so the application must still
    define android:windowOptOutEdgeToEdgeEnforcement so that this works
    properly on Android 15! Earlier Android versions don't enable
    edge-to-edge display by default, so no need to do anything there.
    
    Implementation-wise, this installs a listener for "insets" on the root
    layout and passes the dimensions into Qt so that it can use them for the
    platform screen's available geometry. The existing code played pretty
    loose with the geometry and available geometry since they were always
    the same, so some things had to be shifted over from one to the other.

